I joined the LTV team this year, and almost everything was new to me: the insurance domain, the pipelines, Domino, and later Fabric and Power BI. I learned so much this year.

On the work side, I updated Classic SPL and Renters with the new Finance expense files and added validation to the pipeline so the numbers get checked on every run. I refit the loss ratios with cat losses excluded. I did my first releases, Classic SPL v9.4 and Renters v1.5, and presented the changes at the release parties. After that I worked on the Renters dedup and the condo removal for Classic v10. I also worked on retention model updates, and I helped move our work to Fabric by running the Classic scoring end to end and setting up the weekly schedule.

I also improved our model monitoring. I redesigned the monitoring dashboards so they're consistent and easier to read, and replaced the old drift section with a refit recommendation that updates on its own based on the data. Along the way I found and fixed a bug that made the drift checks flag columns that weren't real model features, and a false alarm on the Classic dashboard caused by a missing table reference. Now the dashboards give us a more reliable picture of when a model needs attention.

I worked closely with the team to get things done and tried to share what I learned along the way. I added lessons learned to the release steps doc and documented releases on Loop. I also learned not to trust a result just because a job ran without errors. A few times things looked fine but weren't, so now I make a point of checking my results. I send Yinan weekly updates so she knows where things stand. I also took her feedback on communication and on committing my work more often; I've improved on both and will keep working on it.

I encountered a number of challenges this year, but I was able to overcome them, and I've grown my skill set and knowledge a lot. I'm looking forward to taking on more next year.
